WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:06.839
I don’t think there’s ever been a video
game quite like Spore.

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:11.590
Released in the late 2000s, the game lets
you create and then guide a lifeform from

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:18.320
a microorganism in a tidepool to an advanced
creature in a galaxy-spanning civilization.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.199
Being able to, essentially, design and play
as your own speculative creature really blew

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:25.310
my mind when I was younger.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:30.689
I remember spending hours trying to make my
creations as detailed as possible, and coming

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:35.730
up with fake scientific attributes about how
they functioned in the ecosystems around them.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.650
Of course, you could also use Spore to create
bizarre monstrosities, and throw the rulebook

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.000
of biology out the window.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.890
After all, the final game was certainly not
always a, uh, scientifically accurate depiction

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:53.860
of evolution, and often cartoonishly over-exaggerated
the stages of life.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:59.200
I also know a lot of content was cut during
its development, which disappointed some.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.470
Yet for all its eccentricities, Spore remains
an evolution simulator that’s quite nostalgic

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.240
to many — myself included.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.470
And the game does explore a number of the
principles of evolution and biology, albeit

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.149
in a simplified manner.

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:17.729
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
make a creature and play through the game

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:22.330
ourselves to gain an understanding of how
this virtual race for survival relates to

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:23.899
real scientific laws.

00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:28.210
And as an added challenge, we’ll try to
keep our creature within the parameters of

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.960
plausibility — as much as such a thing is
possible in Spore.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:39.490
So, let’s begin our multi-billion-year voyage
through this simulated universe…

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:43.429
The game opens on a swirling spiral galaxy.

00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:48.630
The opening screen really sells the potential
of the journey you’re about to embark on.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.439
From this distant perspective, anything seems
possible.

00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:57.819
We have a number of gleaming planets upon
which we can begin our odyssey of adaptation.

00:01:57.819 --> 00:02:00.630
This particular world seems like a good fit.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.390
Like Earth, it seems to have primarily green
plants to match the light wavelengths of its

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:06.390
host star.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:11.750
It’s worth noting that in Spore, as in our
own universe, alien planets might have completely

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.290
different colored foliage depending on their
star’s wavelengths.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:22.500
We’ll call this world ‘Planet Curious,’
and kick off the simulation.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:28.070
The game begins with a sequence of an asteroid
hurtling past the planet’s host star, and

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.970
towards the planet itself.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:35.200
Breaking through the atmosphere and raining
down into the sea, the asteroid carries with

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.990
it hidden microorganisms.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:43.310
The concept of microorganisms surviving in
space for long periods might sound like science

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:49.520
fiction, but certain species of tardigrades,
or ‘water bears,’ can indeed survive the

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.690
vacuum of outer space.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:56.600
The idea that a similar microorganism could
hitch a ride to another planet on an asteroid

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.840
is a genuine scientific theory.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.880
And with the lifeform we’ll be guiding emerging
from the rubble, the first stage of Spore

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.810
has officially begun.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:10.150
While this section of the game is called ‘Cell
Stage,’ it seems to be a multi-cellular

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.460
organism from the very beginning.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:17.650
Our species begins life on this planet drifting
through the tidepool, feeding on pieces of

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.600
algae-like plant matter.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.750
On Earth, many tardigrades live their lives
in a similar manner, locating and feeding

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.440
on clumps of wayward algae cells for sustenance.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.970
As a heterotrophic lifeform, our microorganism
cannot produce food within their body, and

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.730
must regularly consume plant matter to continue
functioning.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.900
Our herbivore’s mouth appears to be that
of a filter feeder, and resembles that of

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.730
Rotifers on Earth.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:48.960
Rotifers are among Earth’s most successful
microscopic filter feeders, vacuuming up plant

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.230
matter using their swirling mouth-parts.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:56.220
And if our microorganism wants to get lunch,
they’ll have to be the first in a rapidly

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.830
expanding line.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:03.670
For other emerging herbivores are after the
same resources, and when resources are limited,

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.150
competition begins.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:11.560
And so, within this tidepool, the first race
for survival on Planet Curious is underway...

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:17.540
If our lifeform is going to survive, they
need to adapt.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:22.010
Entering the cell editor, we can see that
thus far, we’ve been navigating the tidepool

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:27.660
using a single flagellum: a long, whip-like
structure that a wide range of microorganisms

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.540
use for locomotion.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:34.560
Some microorganisms actually possess multiple
flagella to propel themselves along — a

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.320
strategy we’ll copy with our lifeform.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.570
Our species also evolves a pair of lateral
spikes to fend off the competition.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:47.350
Spikes are an extremely basic adaptation,
found on the microscopic level in life like

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:53.570
the heliozoa, whose bodies are surrounded
by sharp projections that can spear other

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.430
microorganisms.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:05:00.520
Now that our lifeform is looking rather promising,
it’s time we give this particular iteration

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.820
a name.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.960
For now, we’ll call them Curiosus Minimus.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.000
Over the course of our multi-billion-year
journey, we’ll see them change quite a bit…

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.750
Back in the tidepool, our extra flagella have
helped us outmaneuver other herbivores.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:21.370
Over the eons, our species has grown larger,
and can now feed on the ends of bigger aquatic

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:25.710
plant structures — again, just like certain
tardigrades.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.420
But it’s no longer just herbivores within
this primordial battleground.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:35.430
At every turn, newly emergent carnivores try
to snap at Curiosus Minimus.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.980
These early hunters resemble some of the oldest
predators in Earth’s fossil record from

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.530
the Cambrian era.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:46.210
Nectocaris is a notable example of an early
carnivore — and really does look like a

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.300
Spore creature in most reconstructions.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:54.419
With all sorts of unfriendly customers swarming
the waters, the spikes of Curiosus Minimus

00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:57.520
are an invaluable adaptation.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.870
Over millions of years, a new trait arises
in the form of navigational appendages the

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:04.960
game calls cilia.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.430
On Earth, the term ‘cilia’ refers to hair-like
structures found on the surface of various

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.090
single-celled organisms.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:17.870
In Spore, cilia are clearly something different,
analogous instead to the tiny, primitive fin

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:20.669
structures present on certain ichthyoplankton.

00:06:20.669 --> 00:06:26.530
And with our new fins, the tidepool has never
been easier to navigate.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.919
So far, we’ve subsisted entirely off plants.

00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:34.949
But in the interest of documenting a wide
variety of behaviors, I’m going to steer

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:38.070
our lifeform down the path of the omnivore.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:44.330
In Spore, this is possible through the development
of a proboscis, or proboscis as it’s sometimes

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:45.330
pronounced.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:51.230
On Earth, various animals feed using a proboscis,
both above the waves and in tidepool-like

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:57.080
environments, as is the case for the Phyllodoce
lineata, a species of worm that uses its strange

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.020
mouth to feed on smaller organisms.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:05.169
With our new mouth, would-be attackers are
now a potential source of nutrition.

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:10.140
Over the next several million years, our species
flourishes — developing a second pair of

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:15.060
fins for navigation, and earning the new name
of Curiosus Aquaticus.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:20.710
We’re now a far larger lifeform than when
we first began, more comparable to a small

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:22.440
fish than a microorganism.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:28.590
And other lifeforms have followed suit, with
life on Planet Curious becoming far more sophisticated

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:29.810
in general.

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:36.250
Some organisms can even deliver a sudden electric
shock if Curiosus Aquaticus gets too close.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:41.680
Mastery over electricity is familiar in our
world, with electric eels using disc-shaped

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.540
cells packed into specialized organs to generate
their painful zaps.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:51.870
While this is a promising strategy, Curiosus
Aquaticus is doing just fine without it — with

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.310
our back fins becoming larger and well-defined.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:58.580
The age of the tidepool is about to come to
an end…

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:03.500
A cartoonish animation shows us that our species
has developed a brain.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:09.199
On earth, the first brains emerged from interconnected
nerve nets, simple nervous systems that are

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:11.630
still present in some types of jellyfish.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:17.169
With our developing brain and enviable omnivore
niche, when it comes to swimming in sludge,

00:08:17.169 --> 00:08:19.660
Curiosus Aquaticus rules the pool.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:24.229
But a new stage of life is about to begin
on dry land…

00:08:24.229 --> 00:08:28.560
Looking back at our history, we’ve already
had quite a journey: from simple herbivore

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.930
to complex omnivore.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:35.950
And now, the next stage in our evolutionary
journey is evolving legs and crawling out

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:36.950
of the water.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:41.690
The land-to-ocean transition process was a
complicated one on Earth, and we don’t see

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:46.430
much of it in Spore, although we can speculate
as to how it happens.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:52.300
Over millions of years, the fins of Curiosus
Aquaticus likely became larger and more muscular

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:57.740
as ecological conditions encouraged them to
spend more and more time at the water’s

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:58.740
edge.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:03.120
These early explorers likely weren’t the
most graceful on land, and still lived their

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.980
lives very much tied to water, like earth
mudskippers.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.570
But eventually, primitive legs emerged.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:15.020
And now, with a fresh new color scheme, the
rather strange lifeform we’ll dub Curiosus

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:21.810
Irregularius is ready to leave the water behind,
and take their first steps as a proper land-dweller.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:26.370
As we enter Spore’s Creature Stage, a loading
screen informs us of the kinds of creatures

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:27.710
we might encounter.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:31.920
And as always, it looks to be quite an unusual
bunch.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:37.640
Curiosus Irregularius starts off life on land
living in small, communal nests, and generally

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.190
lying low.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:44.510
On Earth, there are numerous types of species
that build their nests directly on the ground.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:49.260
But it’s an exciting sign of rising intelligence
that our creature has evolved to live in social

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:50.410
units.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:56.570
As a generalist omnivore, Curiosus Irregularius
spends much of their time foraging for food,

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:59.750
and scuttling along, unseen, through the grasses.

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.660
And they’re not alone on land.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:08.050
Other species — perhaps distant relatives
— have also made the land to water transition

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.040
during this early period.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:15.050
The game gives us the option to hunt them,
or try and impress them socially using displays

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.030
like singing and dancing.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:23.230
On Earth, all manner of animals conduct similarly
unusual displays — but typically only within

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:26.490
their own species to facilitate courtship
and mating.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:31.440
But while this kind of cross-species communication
isn’t likely, it’s the system the game

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.170
provides us with, so we’ll have Curiosus
Irregularius show off their singing voice

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:38.910
to any creature who will listen.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:43.660
Over the generations, Curiosus Irregularius
will change quite a bit.

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:47.760
Within the creature editor, Spore gives us
the option to make pretty much any changes

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:48.820
we want.

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:53.760
Like I said before, you can really push your
creature to some pretty absurd limits.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:58.750
I remember spending hours when I was younger
just testing the boundaries of what Spore

00:10:58.750 --> 00:10:59.920
allowed you to do.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:05.230
While all that is fun, it’s not quite how
evolution works, so we’ll be keeping changes

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:06.230
gradual.

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:10.600
On that note, one ancestral trait I’ve decided
to retain are the stalk eyes.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:15.910
Eyes at the ends of stalks have their drawbacks,
but they also have their advantages, like

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:17.400
a better field of vision.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:20.030
And frankly, they’re just fun.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:25.240
In nature, stalk eye users include certain
crabs, and types of snails, so they’re not

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.360
an unheard-of characteristic.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.279
In terms of a mouth, this leech-like suction
mouth ringed with primitive teeth seems like

00:11:32.279 --> 00:11:35.399
the most likely to emerge from a proboscis.

00:11:35.399 --> 00:11:39.860
Another useful adaptation Spore offers is
a type of projectile venom.

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:45.610
I feel the closest analog to this in the natural
world is the chemical spray of the spitting

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:49.420
cobra, so I’ve placed the poison launcher
in our creature’s mouth.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.470
We can say it’s derived from some kind of
salivary gland, as that’s where most venom

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:56.040
in nature emerges from.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:01.370
Another shift that might feasibly occur over
millions of years is a change in our creature’s

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:02.370
coloration.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:06.960
Colors and patterns play a fascinating role
across Earth’s ecosystems.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.810
Some have bright colors as a signal to others
of their kind, or as a warning to predators.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:17.060
Others have colors that match their environments
to hide or to stalk prey.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:21.370
Still others have colors and patterns that
resemble other forms of life to reduce their

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.370
chance of being targeted.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:28.290
For our purposes, I’ve decided to stick
with a green pattern that matches our environment.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:32.890
And watching our creature in motion, I have
to say that while I endeavored to make a vaguely

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.899
plausible species, they look a bit horrifying.

00:12:35.899 --> 00:12:38.650
I’m still quite attached to them, though.

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:44.620
As ages continue to pass, our species grows
more adjusted to land and begins living in

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:46.470
larger social groups.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:51.900
Other species on Planet Curious are also changing
and diversifying, some of whom we impress

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:55.980
with our social displays, and others who must
be fended off.

00:12:55.980 --> 00:13:01.041
We are still an omnivore, after all, and with
our gnashing teeth and venomous spray, we

00:13:01.041 --> 00:13:03.279
represent a formidable threat.

00:13:03.279 --> 00:13:08.149
Yet we are a food source for some larger,
more specialized carnivores.

00:13:08.149 --> 00:13:13.990
If our species is really going to thrive,
we might have to change our solitary hunting

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.270
strategy…

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:20.459
Another age passes, and we can evolve a larger
mouth with more defined pseudo-teeth.

00:13:20.459 --> 00:13:24.920
Like many of the mouths in Spore, this one
does look… a bit odd.

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:29.410
I do think it’s cool, however, that the
abilities of these mouths seem to be loosely

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:34.620
inspired by the behavior of real-world animals
— with the songbird-like mouth better at

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.320
things like singing, and the crocodile-like
mouth featuring one of the strongest bites.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:44.740
It’s obviously a simplification, but it’s
a fun detail nonetheless.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:49.610
Sticking with our horrifying leech mouth for
now, we can also upgrade our poison spit and

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:54.110
our talons, and add a display structure on
our creatures’ shoulders that will help

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:55.130
us show off.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:14:00.170
I imagine this feather-like structure evolved
from some kind of rigid plate for the purpose

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:01.779
of attracting a mate.

00:14:01.779 --> 00:14:05.990
And while that might sound far-fetched, some
display structures on Earth which have evolved

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.910
to attract mates are far more elaborate.

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:13.380
With a new pattern of stripes on our creature’s
legs to help it blend into the tall grasses,

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:18.209
I think it’s time we gave this iteration
the new name of Curiosus Striatus.

00:14:18.209 --> 00:14:23.430
And this new chapter looks to be an exciting
one, as our species’ social intelligence

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:28.820
has progressed to the point where Curiosus
Striatus can hunt in a coordinated pack.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:33.910
With this critical advantage, our species
is now frequently able to outcompete lineages

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:36.450
that were once higher in the food web.

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:40.260
On occasion, the pack wanders into more trouble
that it can handle.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:45.691
At one point, a group of Curiosus Striatus
are crushed under the foot of an Epic, a type

00:14:45.691 --> 00:14:50.840
of giant monster that I remember being one
of the scarier parts of Spore when I was younger.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:56.120
At another point, we wander out to sea and
are swallowed by a creature from below — another

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.630
part of Spore that I distinctly remember finding
terrifying.

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:04.529
But with each new generation, our pack-hunting
omnivores are getting smarter…

00:15:04.529 --> 00:15:09.740
And as the pages of evolution continue to
turn, it’s possible that Curiosus Striatus

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:11.390
might become bipedal.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:17.380
Bipedalism is a form of terrestrial locomotion
where an organism moves by means of its two

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:19.000
rear limbs.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:23.910
While we associate this kind of walking with
humans, all sorts of animals do it for all

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:25.550
sorts of reasons.

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:30.970
In dinosaurs like the theropods, for example,
bipedalism is thought to have emerged to free

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:35.240
up their front limbs and allow them to grasp
fast moving prey.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:40.480
We can imagine that primitive grasping digits
might emerge in our species for the same reason.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:46.270
With a new mouth and more complex display
structures, this is a big shift for our lifeform.

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:50.709
And as is sometimes the case in Spore, while
our design might look pretty streamlined while

00:15:50.709 --> 00:15:54.660
standing still, in motion, it looks… a bit
unusual.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:55.660
Oh well.

00:15:55.660 --> 00:16:00.800
With a new name to celebrate its grasping
digits, Curiosus Digitatus is born.

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:06.149
And with our new hands, fruit that was once
out of reach is now quite accessible.

00:16:06.149 --> 00:16:12.019
The social interactions of Curiosus Digitatus
are also more complex, moving in larger packs

00:16:12.019 --> 00:16:14.660
across the vast open plains.

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:19.860
With our coordinated movements and theropod-like
anatomy, we’ve never been higher up on the

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:21.990
food web than we are right now.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:27.880
Some Curiosus Digitatus will even, on occasion,
pick up sticks and hold them in their hands.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:32.589
While for the moment this is just the product
of curiosity, it’s similar to the behavior

00:16:32.589 --> 00:16:36.260
of early primates that gave rise to the first
hominids.

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:41.170
With these grasping appendages encouraging
their inquisitiveness, Curiosus Digitatus

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:44.990
are on the cusp of evolving higher intelligence.

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:48.329
Curiosus Digitatus now live in larger nests.

00:16:48.329 --> 00:16:53.260
They can impress just about any species through
their displays, and hunt just about any species

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:56.380
with their claws, fangs, and venomous spit.

00:16:56.380 --> 00:17:01.029
And with another cartoonish graphic conveying
that our brains have once again become larger

00:17:01.029 --> 00:17:05.049
in size, the final stage in our development
is upon us.

00:17:05.049 --> 00:17:10.040
It’s worth noting that while in Spore, your
creature evolves along a direct path toward

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:14.929
advanced intelligence, real evolution is of
course more meandering, with organisms adapting

00:17:14.929 --> 00:17:20.150
to physical changes in their environment,
and not necessarily increasing in intelligence

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:21.150
size over time.

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:25.720
But having a more streamlined journey through
evolution makes Spore more exciting.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:31.660
Speaking of which, entering the creature creator
one last time, we can make the final upgrades.

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:36.110
These include a four-part mouth, which is
similar in some ways to the mouthparts of

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:37.370
certain insects.

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:43.100
Our fingers are also longer and more dexterous
— with the critical beginnings of an opposable,

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:48.010
thumb-like digit — and our display structures
are more extravagant than ever.

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:50.970
Finally, our posture has become more upright.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:56.650
This remarkable iteration has earned the name
Curiosus Sapiens.

00:17:56.650 --> 00:18:01.990
Looking onwards towards the future, the various
social units of Curiosus Sapiens will continue

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:05.670
to thrive and achieve greater complexity.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:10.419
These intelligent omnivores have come a long
way from their tiny, filter-feeding starting

00:18:10.419 --> 00:18:11.590
point.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:16.450
Looking back through our history once again,
it’s been a long and winding road to reach

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:17.450
this point.

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:22.590
The stages after cell and creature all about
technological development, so we won’t be

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:26.880
covering them — at least in this video.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:31.630
Watching a creature evolve in Spore remains
a pretty special experience.

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:36.980
Despite the game’s age, I often see people
posting new stuff they’ve made online.

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:41.340
I know that Spore almost had more features
and more realism in it, and while it’s a

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:46.820
shame those elements got cut, the simulated
galaxy we’re left with is still pretty unique.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:51.710
I think part of what keeps people coming back
to this game is that the experience is very

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:53.330
much what you make it.

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:59.300
You can push the creature creator to its absolute
limits and make absurd monstrosities, or you

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:02.299
can try to stay within the lines of plausible
biology.

00:19:02.299 --> 00:19:03.660
It’s up to you.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:08.780
I’d love to see another high-profile game
tackle something similar, as I feel there’s

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:13.380
real potential for a modern, more science-based
take on this concept.

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:19.549
But for now, we can always fall back on the
weird and wonderful possibilities of Spore.

00:19:19.549 --> 00:19:21.840
As always, thanks for watching.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:26.260
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support to help make these videos possible

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:32.540
by liking, subscribing, and hitting the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:33.880
See you in the next video.

